What is std::shared_mutex?
std::shared_mutex was introduced in C++17. It supports two locking modes:
- Shared/read lock — multiple threads may hold it simultaneously.
- Exclusive/write lock — exactly one thread may hold it, and no readers can hold it simultaneously.
Conceptually:

                std::shared_mutex

        ┌────────────────────────────┐
        │                            │
Readers │ R1   R2   R3   R4   ...   │  ← Allowed concurrently
        │                            │
        └────────────────────────────┘

                    OR

        ┌────────────────────────────┐
Writer  │             W1             │  ← Exclusive
        └────────────────────────────┘

This makes it useful for read-heavy shared state.

Basic example
Imagine a cache that is read very frequently but updated occasionally.

#include <iostream>
#include <shared_mutex>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <string>

class Cache {
private:
    std::unordered_map<int, std::string> cache_;
    mutable std::shared_mutex mutex_;

public:
    std::string get(int key) const {
        std::shared_lock<std::shared_mutex> lock(mutex_);

        auto it = cache_.find(key);

        if (it != cache_.end())
            return it->second;

        return "";
    }

    void put(int key, std::string value) {
        std::unique_lock<std::shared_mutex> lock(mutex_);

        cache_[key] = std::move(value);
    }
};

The important distinction is:

std::shared_lock<std::shared_mutex>

for readers versus

std::unique_lock<std::shared_mutex>

for writers.
If threads execute:

T1 → get()
T2 → get()
T3 → get()
T4 → get()

they can execute concurrently:

Time ───────────────────────────────>

T1  [------ READ ------]
T2     [------ READ ------]
T3        [------ READ ------]
T4          [------ READ ------]

But suppose a writer arrives:

T1  [---- READ ----]
T2  [------- READ -------]

T3                    [--- WRITE ---]

T4                                    [--- READ ---]

The writer must wait until existing readers release their shared locks. While the writer holds the exclusive lock, readers cannot acquire a shared lock.

Why not just use std::mutex?
You absolutely can.
std::mutex mutex_;

std::string get(int key)
{
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
    return cache_[key];
}

But every reader becomes serialized:

std::mutex

Reader 1    [READ]
Reader 2          [READ]
Reader 3                [READ]
Reader 4                      [READ]

Even though none of those operations modifies the data.
With shared_mutex:

Reader 1    [--------- READ ---------]
Reader 2      [--------- READ ---------]
Reader 3        [--------- READ ---------]
Reader 4          [--------- READ ---------]


So the potential gain comes from reader concurrency.

The three APIs you should know
A shared_mutex itself exposes:

mutex.lock();
mutex.unlock();

mutex.lock_shared();
mutex.unlock_shared();

Normally, don't manually call these.
Use RAII.
For exclusive access:

std::unique_lock<std::shared_mutex> lock(mutex);

For shared access:

std::shared_lock<std::shared_mutex> lock(mutex);

What is std::shared_mutex, and when would you use it?

std::shared_mutex is a C++17 reader-writer synchronization primitive supporting shared and exclusive ownership. Multiple threads can simultaneously acquire shared ownership, 
typically through std::shared_lock, while a writer acquires exclusive ownership, typically through std::unique_lock.
I'd consider it for read-heavy shared structures such as caches or configuration stores where serialization caused by a regular mutex is measurably limiting throughput.
I wouldn't assume it's automatically faster than std::mutex, because reader-writer locks have additional synchronization overhead and their benefit depends on contention, 
critical-section duration and the read/write ratio.
I'd also consider writer latency and fairness, avoid unsafe read-to-write upgrade assumptions, and revalidate state if I release a shared lock before obtaining exclusive ownership.
At larger scale, I'd question whether one shared mutex is itself the bottleneck and consider sharding, immutable snapshots, atomics, or other synchronization strategies
depending on the access pattern.

